PIPELINE EVIDENCE:

The production run records two historical gate failures: length (781 words, below the 800-word floor) and seo-subheader (1 of the required 2 distinctive SEO-term words appearing across subheadings). The current manuscript at 830 words resolves the length gate. The deterministic standards record â€” the authoritative reading â€” shows publishable: true and no findings, confirming all gates cleared. The seo-subheader gate historically failed; the addition of "## Extraction Over Consent" as a subheading, which places the word "Extraction" from the SEO term in a subheader, is the likely mechanism by which the subheader count moved. Both historical failures were draft-layer corrections. The writer model produced a structurally adequate piece that ran short; the repair added volume through a substantive section on AI, data sovereignty, and the UNDRIP anniversary rather than padding earlier sections with repetition.

---

PIPELINE IMPROVEMENT:

The replay architecture correctly identified the writer layer as the origin point for a length deficit, and the repair preserved factual integrity throughout â€” the expansion in section four draws on material actually present in the source rather than inflating claims. This is the desired replay behavior.

One structural note for future runs involving this type of SEO term: "extraction and the global south" poses a subheader challenge because "global south" as a literal phrase does not appear in the subject source. The geographic scope is expressed through specifics â€” Bangladesh, New Caledonia, Botswana, Ontario First Nations â€” rather than the category label. The writer model handled this correctly by not forcing the phrase into an awkward subheader. Pipeline designers should note that when the SEO demand term contains a geographic abstraction absent from the source packet, the seo-subheader gate may require additional passes not because the model underperforms but because the gate counts literal tokens against prose where the concept is properly expressed through concrete particulars. The current piece resolved this correctly; no pipeline change is warranted here, but the pattern is worth noting for similar assignments.

