r","charge":"fail","evidence":"1/5 distinctive demand word(s) from \"extraction and the global south\" appear naturally in one ## subheader (4 subheaders; needs 2)"},{"kind":"producing-content.gate.charge","gate":"seo-frequency","charge":"pass","evidence":"SEO term appears 0Ãƒâ€” (Ã¢â€°Â¤ 5)"},{"kind":"producing-content.gate.charge","gate":"citations","charge":"pass","evidence":"story cited, 0 supporting citation(s), all within the witnessed intake"},{"kind":"producing-content.gate.charge","gate":"no-prophecy","charge":"pass","evidence":"no certainty about the future"},{"kind":"producing-content.gate.charge","gate":"repetition","charge":"pass","evidence":"no repeated sentences or 8-word phrases"},{"kind":"producing-content.gate.charge","gate":"summary-length","charge":"pass","evidence":"summary 53 words (needs 40-70 Ã¢â‚¬â€ three or four FULL sentences, like the exemplar, never fragments); description 138 chars (needs 100-150 Ã¢â‚¬â€ one full sentence plus a clause)"},{"kind":"producing-content.gate.charge","gate":"title-fidelity","charge":"pass","evidence":"every quoted source title matches the witnessed intake exactly"},{"kind":"producing-content.gate.charge","gate":"template-monotony","charge":"pass","evidence":"0 \"As [source] notes/reports/suggests/writes, Ã¢â‚¬Â¦\" sentence(s) (at most 2; vary how sources enter the prose)"},{"kind":"producing-content.gate.charge","gate":"prose","charge":"pass","evidence":"no prose-lint errors"},{"kind":"producing-content.gate.charge","gate":"numbers-faithfulness","charge":"pass","evidence":"all draft numbers appear in the witnessed intake"},{"kind":"producing-content.gate.charge","gate":"extras","charge":"pass","evidence":"description, summary, and tweet present"}],"usage":{"calls":1,"tokens":6199},"inference":{"provider":"nim","model":"qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b"},"composition":{"prompt":"You are Nik Koios, Editor-in-Chief of Koios.News, a collapse-aware news publication.\nYour frame: climate breakdown is the upstream cause of the other macro crises; no event is isolated;\ncollapse is a dynamically compounding emergent phenomenon, not a series of escalating crises.\nYour voice: grounded, specific, humane; confident-but-comfortable opinionation; concise; educational;\none or two examples per point, never ten; never breathless, never prophetic.\n\nSEARCH-DEMAND LENS (a subject signal, not mandatory copy): \"extraction and the global south\"\nExpress the lens's material ideas in natural language. Do not force its exact wording into a sentence, headline, subheading, description, summary, or share copy when that wording is awkward or unsupported.\n\nFORM EXEMPLAR (match the SHAPE Ã¢â‚¬â€ a lede that names the event, sections that build one analysis, concrete numbers Ã¢â‚¬â€ never reuse its words):\n  Lede: \"In a move that's rattling global markets, Thailand has abruptly halted rice exports due to an escalating water crisis, prompting fears of a new wave of food price surges and shortages. This isn't just a Thai issue. It's a warning flare for the whole world. The Thailand rice export ban is a fracture in the illusion of a globally integrated food system.\"\n  Description: \"Thailand's rice export ban amid drought signals a global food system in breakdown. Here's how it affects us all.\" (113 characters)\n  Summary (three full sentences, 53 words Ã¢â‚¬â€ the event, the numbers, the frame): \"Thailand's rice export ban due to historic drought is triggering global price spikes and exposing the fragility of our food systems. This collapse in rice trade reflects a deeper crisis: climate volatility is unraveling global agriculture. The illusion of stable food supply chains is breakingÃ¢â‚¬â€one drought, one policy, at a time.\"\n\nTHE STORY (the single subject Ã¢â‚¬â€ the WHOLE article is about this one event):\n\nAs climate and conflict collide, Indigenous leaders confront overlapping crises at the UN\n    URL: https://ictnews.org/news/as-climate-and-conflict-collide-indigenous-leaders-confront-overlapping-crises-at-the-un/\n    PUBLISHED: Thu, 16 Jul 2026 15:34:13 +0000\n    SOURCE TEXT:\n    This story was originally published by Grist. Te Aniwaniwa Paterson & Dionne Phillips Grist This story is published through the Indigenous News Alliance. Although itâ€™s been nearly 20 years since more than 100 countries adopted the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples, the rights and lives of those it is meant to protect remain under constant threat from compounding crises. Indigenous lands are battered by record-breaking storms , dessicated by drought, and vanishing under rising seas. Amid these climate-driven threats, Indigenous peoples continue dying by the hundreds in wars and while defending their lands , even as they are persecuted by government officials . â€œClimate change, militarization, extractivism, and legal marginalization reinforce one another,â€ said Binota Moy Dhamai, who is Tripura from the Chittagong Hill Tracts in Bangladesh, and a former chair of the United Nations Expert Mechanism on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples, or EMRIP . This week, hundreds of Indigenous delegates gather in Geneva at the 19th annual gathering of that United Nations body to discuss implementing solutions to these abuses and push all countries to more proactively protect Indigenous peoples everywhere. EMRIP, one of three U.N. organizations focused exclusively on Indigenous peoples, is composed of seven Indigenous experts, each representing a different region. As part of the U.N. Human Rights Council, they focus on developing the data, recommendations, and advice to shape international standards regarding the fundamental rights of Indigenous peoples and support their enforcement. This yearâ€™s session will see Indigenous leaders, governments, and experts come together for a week of discussions and debates. The weekâ€™s meetings will cover both longstanding and emerging issues, including inadequate disaster relief, artificial intelligence, and resource extraction. For many Indigenous leaders around the world, the stakes could not be any higher, given the overlapping existential threats of violent conflict and climate change. â€œIndigenous peoples always face the brunt of conflict,â€ said Valmaine Toki, who is MÄori and the EMRIP member representing the Pacific region. Conflict, she said, leads to the dispossession of, and alienation from, their traditional lands even as the people from whom they are stolen are drawn into those fights. â€œItâ€™s a lose-lose situation for Indigenous peoples on all fronts.â€ This year, those meeting in Geneva face a growing list of intersecting issues, including militarization, climate change, exploitation by extractive industries, biodiversity decline, shrinking civic space, and digital transformation, Dhamai said. For Indigenous peoples in the Pacific, for instance, climate change and colonization are inextricably linked. Those from the South Pacific nation of New Caledonia have been part of the Pacific-led effort to hold large countries responsible for the climate crisis and its impact. Viro Xulue is Kanak and the Human Rights and Indigenous Rights advisor for the Drehu Customary Council, which is a traditional Kanak governance body. He said such work is part of the ongoing fight for true independence. â€œItâ€™s important for us in the process of decolonization because itâ€™s to respect the self-determination and what we want for our future, what we want for our children, and what we want to protect our resources, our moana, and our dream for the next generation,â€ Xulue said. Despite legal victories like an International Court of Justice advisory opinion that said the countries contributing to climate change are accountable for the damage it has caused, the Indigenous people of New Caledonia continue fighting for their inherent rights. â€œThe inequity for Kanak people is always here since 170 years of colonization,â€ Xulue said. â€œThe injustice is always here for the Indigenous Kanak. They didnâ€™t resolve the problem of the colonization and the impact of the colonization.â€ Although the challenges facing Indigenous peoples vary across regions, those attending EMRIP plan to discuss several common themes. One of them is the ongoing fight for Indigenous involvement in government decision-making. Many countries do not follow international standards, and there is widespread agreement among Indigenous peoples that say they should be consulted and included in discussions that could impact them and their land. In northern Ontario, for example, the Canadian government has granted mining rights on the vast wetland ecosystem known as the Breathing Lands. It is part of the Ring of Fire mineral deposit and holds enormous stores of nickel and other metals. It is also a massive carbon sink and a key biodiversity hub. Extraction projects are proceeding despite strong resistance from First Nations in the area, including the Nishnawbe Aski Nation, that represents 49 communities. Kohen Mattinas of Lac Seul First Nation and Constance Lake First Nation is representing Nishnawbe Aski Nation at EMRIP and says that the right of all Indigenous peoples to free, prior, and informed consent must be respected. Mattinas is also the co-founder of Okiniwak, a youth organization created in 2025 in response to Canadian legislation that allows projects to be â€œadvanced through an accelerated process.â€ In addition to its international advocacy, Okiniwak organizes rallies in Indigenous communities and at legislative buildings. â€œWeâ€™ve taken it upon ourselves to protect the land and our rights on the front lines,â€ he said. For Mattinas, attending EMRIP is an important part of that work. â€œThrough speaking at these international forums, weâ€™re also able to connect with other Indigenous people around the world who are either facing the same things, or they face similar things, and we just kind of learn from each other, bring back that knowledge home, or we are able to collaborate in ways to strengthen both of our messaging or our work,â€ he said. Those connections can be even more valuable for Indigenous people from countries that do not recognize their legal status, like Botswana. Nichodimas Cooper, who is Indigenous Nama from that nation, said that lack of recognition makes it difficult to bring change on the national level. Cooper said his people face forced assimilation and displacement, and EMRIP offers a unique opportunity to pressure countries to respect their inalienable rights. â€œWe find it as a very important platform that empowers Indigenous peoples like us,â€ he said. â€œWe hardly have such a privilege as Indigenous communities to resource ourselves to attend these platforms and be able to inform ourselves on the mechanisms or platforms that are able to make impact within our advocacyâ€™s hope.â€ But as Indigenous delegates continue building solidarity, new threats, like artificial intelligence, are emerging. That has led to a push for data sovereignty and stronger protections against technologies being developed without free, prior, and informed consent. Dhamai said he hopes EMRIP will provide guidance on these mounting issues, alongside climate finance and the global energy transition, because Indigenous perspectives must shape international standards rather than respond to them. This yearâ€™s session is also expected to be shaped by reflections on the approaching 20th anniversary of UNDRIP next year. Nearly two decades after the declarationâ€™s adoption, the central question facing delegates remains remarkably similar to the one asked in 2007: not whether Indigenous rights should be recognized, but whether governments are prepared to uphold them. The post As climate and conflict collide, Indigenous leaders confront overlapping crises at the UN appeared first on ICT .\n\nSUPPORTING CONTEXT (separate reporting, not evidence that its people, region, causes, or conclusions are the same as THE STORY):\n\n[CONTEXT 1] Africaâ€™s Greater Horn region is facing a looming polycrisis fueled by conflict, prices, climate and disease\n    URL: https://theconversation.com/africas-greater-horn-region-is-facing-a-looming-polycrisis-fueled-by-conflict-prices-climate-and-disease-286071\n    PUBLISHED: 2026-07-16T12:22:02Z\n    SOURCE TEXT:\n    <figure><img src=\"https://images.theconversation.com/files/748013/original/file-20260715-69-57ggpf.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&rect=0%2C0%2C4200%2C2800&q=45&auto=format&w=1050&h=700&fit=crop\" /><figcaption><span class=\"caption\">A farmer gathers goat carcasses in drought-hit northeastern Kenya on Jan. 21, 2026.</span> <span class=\"attribution\"><a class=\"source\" href=\"https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/abdirizak-gabow-gathers-goat-carcasses-from-the-surrounding-news-photo/2257847523?adppopup=true\">Tony Karumba/AFP via Getty Images</a></span></figcaption></figure><p>The impacts of a <a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/21/climate/el-nino-history-famine.html?smid=nytcore-ios-share\">strengthening El NiÃ±o</a> and the lingering effects of the <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/despite-trumps-wishes-iran-is-dragging-the-us-into-a-long-war-287163\">war in Iran</a> highlight two separate and seemingly unrelated global hazards. But in the Greater Horn of Africa, an area already <a href=\"https://acleddata.com/region/conflict-horn-africa\">beset by prolonged crisis and conflict</a>, these factors are combining with others to potentially create a major, multicountry polycrisis. </p> <p>As <a href=\"https://nutrition.tufts.edu/academics/faculty/daniel-maxwell\">experts in famine</a>, <a href=\"https://fletcher.tufts.edu/academics/faculty/alex-de-waal\">conflict</a> <a href=\"https://africacenter.org/experts/luka-kuol/\">and</a> <a href=\"https://nutrition.tufts.edu/academics/faculty/merry-fitzpatrick\">humanitarian</a> <a href=\"https://fic.tufts.edu/team/peter-hailey-2/\">crises</a>, our concerns about such a grave risk for the region in the second half of 2026 and into 2027 are based on a series of overlapping trends that include El NiÃ±o, the broader effects on food prices and fertilizer of the Iran war, internal conflicts and other localized factors. We fear the consequences could be catastrophic.</p> <p><iframe id=\"1Ourf\" class=\"tc-infographic-datawrapper\" src=\"https://datawrapper.dwcdn.net/1Ourf/1/\" height=\"400px\" width=\"100%\" style=\"border: 0;\" scrolling=\"no\" frameborder=\"0\" title=\"A map of the Greater Horn of Africa\"></iframe></p> <p>Several factors underpin this risk. </p> <p>First, the short rains in the eastern Greater Horn of Africa â€“ parts of Somalia, eastern Kenya, eastern Ethiopia â€“ were well below average in the <a href=\"https://fsnau.org/downloads/NDVI-Index-for-Somalia-Jan-2007-Apr-2026.pdf\">fall of 2025</a>. The long rains in <a href=\"https://faoswalim.org/resources/site_files/Somalia_Rainfall_Forecast_21_May_2026.pdf\">March to May of 2026</a> were also <a href=\"https://fews.net/east-africa/seasonal-monitor/june-2026/print\">well below normal</a> for much of that area, affecting both crops production and grazing and water for livestock, deepening the crisis of rural livelihoods.</p> <p>The regional forecast for the northern Horn of Africa, where for the most part there is only one rainy season per year, is also projected to be <a href=\"https://www.icpac.net/publications/technical-statement-from-the-73rd-greater-horn-of-africa-climate-outlook-forum-ghacof-73/\">below average</a>. This includes the heavily populated highlands of Ethiopia, much of South Sudan and Sudan.</p> <figure class=\"align-center \"> <img alt=\"A man sits on the ground next to a donkey\" src=\"https://images.theconversation.com/files/748014/original/file-20260715-69-561rdd.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&q=45&auto=format&w=754&fit=clip\" srcset=\"https://images.theconversation.com/files/748014/original/file-20260715-69-561rdd.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&q=45&auto=format&w=600&h=400&fit=crop&dpr=1 600w, https://images.theconversation.com/files/748014/original/file-20260715-69-561rdd.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&q=30&auto=format&w=600&h=400&fit=crop&dpr=2 1200w, https://images.theconversation.com/files/748014/original/file-20260715-69-561rdd.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&q=15&auto=format&w=600&h=400&fit=crop&dpr=3 1800w, https://images.theconversation.com/files/748014/original/file-20260715-69-561rdd.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&q=45&auto=format&w=754&h=503&fit=crop&dpr=1 754w, https://images.theconversation.com/files/748014/original/file-20260715-69-561rdd.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&q=30&auto=format&w=754&h=503&fit=crop&dpr=2 1508w, https://images.theconversation.com/files/748014/original/file-20260715-69-561rdd.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&q=15&auto=format&w=754&h=503&fit=crop&dpr=3 2262w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1466px) 754px, (max-width: 599px) 100vw, (min-width: 600px) 600px, 237px\"> <figcaption> <span class=\"caption\">An elderly man waits to refill his donkey-drawn water tank during a water crisis in Port Sudan on April 9, 2024.</span> <span class=\"attribution\"><a class=\"source\" href=\"https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/an-elderly-man-waits-to-refill-his-donkey-drawn-water-tank-news-photo/2141961731?adppopup=true\">AFP via Getty Images</a></span> </figcaption> </figure> <p>The <a href=\"https://thehill.com/policy/energy-environment/5962792-super-el-nino-now-predicted-to-be-even-stronger-and-longer-how-will-it-impact-you/\">super El NiÃ±o now forming</a> could cause both drought and extensive flooding in parts of the Greater Horn of Africa, causing population displacement and tremendous loss of livelihoods.</p> <p>Second, an unpredictable variant of the <a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON602\">Ebola virus</a> has taken hold in Congo and Uganda. Although it hasnâ€™t yet spread to the rest of the region, the concern is that if Ebola spreads across the border to South Sudan, it will not only add a major epidemic risk but will also render the response to other shocks more difficult.</p> <p>Third is the fact that all of the countries in the Greater Horn, with the exception of Kenya, are experiencing protracted, <a href=\"https://www.crisisgroup.org/sites/default/files/2026-07/on-the-horizon-july-2026-hoa-09vii26.pdf\">violent conflicts</a>, with multiple parties jockeying for power and military advantage. This makes the task of dealing with the impact of climate,\n    [SOURCE TEXT TRUNCATED AFTER 6000 CHARACTERS]\n\nA context item may supply one explicitly attributed fact or comparison only when its own text warrants that use. Never fuse contexts, transfer an attribution between them, infer a shared identity or causal connection, or give a context item a section of its own. Ignore context that does not sharpen the subject story safely.\n\nIf this reporting packet cannot support a specific, fully attributed article without invented connective tissue, output only `REPORTING_NEEDED: <the exact missing reporting>` and stop. Refusal is correct work.\n\nWrite one article about THE STORY Ã¢â‚¬â€ one event, one developed argument, never a roundup:\n- aim for 800 to 1,100 words only when the supplied reporting supports that length; never pad to reach a quota\n- use three to five ## sections sized by the actual reporting, with concrete detail rather than repeated framing\n- the lede (the first one or two paragraphs, 100-150 words) comes BEFORE the first ## subheader\n- the very first sentence names the event concretely (who, where, what) and naturally expresses the search-demand lens when the reporting warrants it\n- never repeat a noun phrase of five or more words: after its first mention, the event takes a short handle (\"the ban\", \"the hike\", \"the retreat\")\n- each section CONTINUES the previous one's argument; never reintroduce or restate the event Ã¢â‚¬â€ assume the reader read the lede\n- three to five ## sections that BUILD a single analysis of this event: what happened and why, where the faultline runs, who absorbs the damage first, what it opens onto Ã¢â‚¬â€ never one section per source\n- use a natural subheading that expresses the search-demand lens when it belongs to the story; exact-match phrasing is never required\n- work concrete details from the source text into the prose Ã¢â‚¬â€ institutions, numbers, places, names; never invent events, numbers, quotes, people, relationships, or causal mechanisms\n- CITATION IS A PRECONDITION: cite THE STORY as [Exact Story Title](its-exact-url) before making its first factual claim; a draft with no exact story link is refused, not finished\n- every factual paragraph must make its reporting source unambiguous through an inline source link or an immediately continuing attribution; cite supporting context only when you actually use a warranted fact from it; never cite anything not listed above\n- names appearing in a byline, copyright notice, syndication notice, or publication header are authors or carriers, not people in the reported event unless the source text separately attributes an action, role, or quotation to them\n- NEVER alter a quoted source title by even one word: link text that quotes a title reproduces it exactly, character for character\n- template sentences are banned: the construction \"As [X] notes/reports, ...\" may appear AT MOST twice in the whole article; vary how sources enter your prose\n- vary the opening sentence of every section; no two sections open with the same construction\n- never repeat a sentence; never reuse the same long phrase; when you need more length, add NEW concrete material from the sources Ã¢â‚¬â€ never restatements of points already made\nread the event through the collapse frame; no section treats it as isolated\n- a vision is never a prophecy: write \"might\" or \"could\". NEVER use the word \"will\" outside a quoted source title\n- no rhetorical questions, anywhere; every sentence is a statement\n- the ending opens outward Ã¢â‚¬â€ what this event makes visible or possible Ã¢â‚¬â€ without predicting anything\n- do not include an h1 title line in the body\n\nOutput EXACTLY this structure (no preamble, no commentary):\nTITLE: <descriptive headline about the story's event>\nDESCRIPTION: <aim for 125 characters of faithful meta description (the gate window is 100-150 characters)>\nSUMMARY: <aim for 55 words in three or four FULL faithful sentences (the gate window is 40-70 words)>\nTWEET: <one faithful share sentence with one emoji, no hashtags>\nTAGS: <three to five comma-separated topic tags>\nBODY:\n<the markdown body>","promptDigest":"sha256:0fa0ac56c02447625a59d0bd66d02f8960176accd2faeb176ffa21a90f83803a","promptCharacters":20390},"references":{"slug":"indigenous-leaders-confront-the-collision-of-climate-and-extraction","path":"/home/emsenn/info/lib/emsenn/services/561-group/services/rsc-quiescent/services/koios-news/sites/koios.news/content/posts/indigenous-leaders-confront-the-collision-of-climate-and-extraction.md","changed":true,"sourceUrlCount":1,"referenceCount":1,"kind":"producing-content.source-cycle.references"},"internalLinks":{"slug":"indigenous-leaders-confront-the-collision-of-climate-and-extraction","path":"/home/emsenn/info/lib/emsenn/services/561-group/services/rsc-quiescent/services/koios-news/sites/koios.news/content/posts/indigenous-leaders-confront-the-collision-of-climate-and-extraction.md","changed":true,"suggestions":[{"targetSlug":"england-sinking-homes-insurance-climate-risk-withdrawal","targetPath":"/posts/england-sinking-homes-insurance-climate-risk-withdrawal","targetHeadline":"England's Sinking Homes Are an Early Notice of Insurance Climate Risk Withdrawal","matchedTerms":["climate breakdown","climate","breakdown"],"confidence":0.57,"markdownLink":"[England's Sinking Homes Are an Early Notice of Insurance Climate Risk Withdrawal](/posts/england-sinking-homes-insurance-climate-risk-withdrawal)","writingGuidance":"Consider this same-site link only if the draft body makes a local, sourced claim about: climate breakdown, climate, breakdown."},{"targetSlug":"hello-world","targetPath":"/posts/hello-world","targetHeadline":"Hello World From Koios.News","matchedTerms":["climate breakdown","climate","breakdown"],"confidence":0.57,"markdownLink":"[Hello World From Koios.News](/posts/hello-world)","writingGuidance":"Consider this same-site link only if the draft body makes a local, sourced claim about: climate breakdown, climate, breakdown."},{"targetSlug":"michigan-program-helps-new-farmers-amidst-rising-crop-failure","targetPath":"/posts/michigan-program-helps-new-farmers-amidst-rising-crop-failure","targetHeadline":"Michigan Program Helps New Farmers Amidst Rising Crop Failure Food Prices","matchedTerms":["climate breakdown","climate","breakdown"],"confidence":0.57,"markdownLink":"[Michigan Program Helps New Farmers Amidst Rising Crop Failure Food Prices](/posts/michigan-program-helps-new-farmers-amidst-rising-crop-failure)","writingGuidance":"Consider this same-site link only if the draft body makes a local, sourced claim about: climate breakdown, climate, breakdown."}],"kind":"producing-content.source-cycle.draft-internal-link-guidance","suggestionCount":3}}}

WITNESSED SOURCE RECORDS
[{"path":"decolonization/africa-s-greater-horn-region-is-facing-a-looming-polycrisis-fueled-by-conflict-prices-climate-and-disease.json","value":{"subject":"africa-s-greater-horn-region-is-facing-a-looming-polycrisis-fueled-by-conflict-prices-climate-and-disease","claims":[{"id":"title","proposition":{"predicate":"title","object":"Africaâ€™s Greater Horn region is facing a looming polycrisis fueled by conflict, prices, climate and disease"},"time":{"asserted":"2026-07-18T04:08:29.665Z"},"force":"alleges","provenance":{"witness":"decolonization","citation":"https://theconversation.com/africas-greater-horn-region-is-facing-a-looming-polycrisis-fueled-by-conflict-prices-climate-and-disease-286071"},"status":"supported"},{"id":"url","proposition":{"predicate":"url","object":"https://theconversation.com/africas-greater-horn-region-is-facing-a-looming-polycrisis-fueled-by-conflict-prices-climate-and-disease-286071"},"time":{"asserted":"2026-07-18T04:08:29.665Z"},"force":"alleges","provenance":{"witness":"decolonization","citation":"https://theconversation.com/africas-greater-horn-region-is-facing-a-looming-polycrisis-fueled-by-conflict-prices-climate-and-disease-286071"},"status":"supported"},{"id":"summary","proposition":{"predicate":"summary","object":"<figure><img src=\"https://images.theconversation.com/files/748013/original/file-20260715-69-57ggpf.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;rect=0%2C0%2C4200%2C2800&amp;q=45&amp;auto=format&amp;w=1050&amp;h=700&amp;fit=crop\" /><figcaption><span class=\"caption\">A farmer gathers goat carcasses in drought-hit northeastern Kenya on Jan. 21, 2026.</span> <span class=\"attribution\"><a class=\"source\" href=\"https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/abdirizak-gabow-gathers-goat-carcasses-from-the-surrounding-news-photo/2257847523?adppopup=true\">Tony Karumba/AFP via Getty Images</a></span></figcaption></figure><p>The impacts of a <a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/21/climate/el-nino-history-famine.html?smid=nytcore-ios-share\">strengthening El NiÃ±o</a> and the lingering effects of the <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/despite-trumps-wishes-iran-is-dragging-the-us-into-a-long-war-287163\">war in Iran</a> highlight two separate and seemingly unrelated global hazards. But in the Greater Horn of Africa, an area already <a href=\"https://acleddata.com/region/conflict-horn-africa\">beset by prolonged crisis and conflict</a>, these factors are combining with others to potentially create a major, multicountry polycrisis. </p> <p>As <a href=\"https://nutrition.tufts.edu/academics/faculty/daniel-maxwell\">experts in famine</a>, <a href=\"https://fletcher.tufts.edu/academics/faculty/alex-de-waal\">conflict</a> <a href=\"https://africacenter.org/experts/luka-kuol/\">and</a> <a href=\"https://nutrition.tufts.edu/academics/faculty/merry-fitzpatrick\">humanitarian</a> <a href=\"https://fic.tufts.edu/team/peter-hailey-2/\">crises</a>, our concerns about such a grave risk for the region in the second half of 2026 and into 2027 are based on a series of overlapping trends that include El NiÃ±o, the broader effects on food prices and fertilizer of the Iran war, internal conflicts and other localized factors. We fear the consequences could be catastrophic.</p> <p><iframe id=\"1Ourf\" class=\"tc-infographic-datawrapper\" src=\"https://datawrapper.dwcdn.net/1Ourf/1/\" height=\"400px\" width=\"100%\" style=\"border: 0;\" scrolling=\"no\" frameborder=\"0\" title=\"A map of the Greater Horn of Africa\"></iframe></p> <p>Several factors underpin this risk. </p> <p>First, the short rains in the eastern Greater Horn of Africa â€“ parts of Somalia, eastern Kenya, eastern Ethiopia â€“ were well below average in the <a href=\"https://fsnau.org/downloads/NDVI-Index-for-Somalia-Jan-2007-Apr-2026.pdf\">fall of 2025</a>. The long rains in <a href=\"https://faoswalim.org/resources/site_files/Somalia_Rainfall_Forecast_21_May_2026.pdf\">March to May of 2026</a> were also <a href=\"https://fews.net/east-africa/seasonal-monitor/june-2026/print\">well below normal</a> for much of that area, affecting both crops production and grazing and water for livestock, deepening the crisis of rural livelihoods.</p> <p>The regional forecast for the northern Horn of Africa, where for the most part there is only one rainy season per year, is also projected to be <a href=\"https://www.icpac.net/publications/technical-statement-from-the-73rd-greater-horn-of-africa-climate-outlook-forum-ghacof-73/\">below average</a>. This includes the heavily populated highlands of Ethiopia, much of South Sudan and Sudan.</p> <figure class=\"align-center \"> <img alt=\"A man sits on the ground next to a donkey\" src=\"https://images.theconversation.com/files/748014/original/file-20260715-69-561rdd.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;q=45&amp;auto=format&amp;w=754&amp;fit=clip\" srcset=\"https://images.theconversation.com/files/748014/original/file-20260715-69-561rdd.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;q=45&amp;auto=format&amp;w=600&amp;h=400&amp;fit=crop&amp;dpr=1 600w, https://images.theconversation.com/files/748014/original/file-20260715-69-561rdd.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;q=30&amp;auto=format&amp;w=600&amp;h=400&amp;fit=crop&amp;dpr=2 1200w, https://images.theconversation.com/files/748014/original/file-20260715-69-561rdd.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;q=15&amp;auto=format&amp;w=600&amp;h=400&amp;fit=crop&amp;dpr=3 1800w, https://images.theconversation.com/files/748014/original/file-20260715-69-561rdd.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;q=45&amp;auto=format&amp;w=754&amp;h=503&amp;fit=crop&amp;dpr=1 754w, https://images.theconversation.com/files/748014/original/file-20260715-69-561rdd.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;q=30&amp;auto=format&amp;w=754&amp;h=503&amp;fit=crop&amp;dpr=2 1508w, https://images.theconversation.com/files/748014/original/file-20260715-69-561rdd.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;q=15&amp;auto=format&amp;w=754&amp;h=503&amp;fit=crop&amp;dpr=3 2262w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1466px) 754px, (max-width: 599px) 100vw, (min-width: 600px) 600px, 237px\"> <figcaption> <span class=\"caption\">An elderly man waits to refill his donkey-drawn water tank during a water crisis in Port Sudan on April 9, 2024.</span> <span class=\"attribution\"><a class=\"source\" href=\"https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/an-elderly-man-waits-to-refill-his-donkey-drawn-water-tank-news-photo/2141961731?adppopup=true\">AFP via Getty Images</a></span> </figcaption> </figure> <p>The <a href=\"https://thehill.com/policy/energy-environment/5962792-super-el-nino-now-predicted-to-be-even-stronger-and-longer-how-will-it-impact-you/\">super El NiÃ±o now forming</a> could cause both drought and extensive flooding in parts of the Greater Horn of Africa, causing population displacement and tremendous loss of livelihoods.</p> <p>Second, an unpredictable variant of the <a href=\"https://www.who.int/emergencies/disease-outbreak-news/item/2026-DON602\">Ebola virus</a> has taken hold in Congo and Uganda. Although it hasnâ€™t yet spread to the rest of the region, the concern is that if Ebola spreads across the border to South Sudan, it will not only add a major epidemic risk but will also render the response to other shocks more difficult.</p> <p>Third is the fact that all of the countries in the Greater Horn, with the exception of Kenya, are experiencing protracted, <a href=\"https://www.crisisgroup.org/sites/default/files/2026-07/on-the-horizon-july-2026-hoa-09vii26.pdf\">violent conflicts</a>, with multiple parties jockeying for power and military advantage. This makes the task of dealing with the impact of climate, health and price shocks that much harder and risks further exacerbating or accelerating individual drivers.</p> <p>Regional analysts have warned of an uptick in violence for <a href=\"https://www.crisisgroup.org/brf/africa/ethiopia-eritrea/b210-ethiopia-eritrea-and-tigray-powder-keg-horn-africa\">Ethiopia</a> and <a href=\"https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker/conflict/civil-war-south-sudan\">South Sudan</a>. Violent conflict is a common denominator of contemporary famine, and famine or the risk of famine has already been <a href=\"https://www.ipcinfo.org/\">noted in several countries</a> in the region. </p> <p>Conflict outside the region is also complicating matters. The war in Iran has already led to <a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/12/briefing/iran-war-costs-inflation.html\">significant increases in transportation and food costs</a> in the short term. But potentially more concerning is the fertilizer shortage caused by the war, which is projected to reduce harvests later in the year. If this happens, food prices will remain high <a href=\"https://www.aljazeera.com/opinions/2026/5/21/the-crises-caused-by-the-iran-war-will-hit-the-world-in-four-waves\">long after the active conflict ends</a>.</p> <p>The Horn of Africa will be doubly affected by the fertilizer bottleneck â€“ local production will be reduced, and global food prices will be higher. The bottleneck affects regional surplus-producing areas that might normally help to even out availability and prices in areas that do not produce enough to feed themselves. The El NiÃ±o effects, in combination with the high fertilizer costs, will likely reduce opportunities for agricultural work while also increasing the amount that food laborers â€“ among the poorest people â€“ have to spend on produce.</p> <p>This combination of climatic, conflict, market and epidemic shocks are not simply additive but multiplicative, with each intensifying the other. Climate and epidemic shocks almost inevitably exacerbate conflict and encourage crackdowns on population movements.</p> <h2>A devastating aid cliff</h2> <p>To make matters worse, there are reasons to believe that the region will be less prepared to cope with the looming crisis than during previous El NiÃ±o years. </p> <p>In the background is the deliberate <a href=\"https://www.cfr.org/articles/great-aid-recession-2025s-humanitarian-crash-nine-charts\">gutting of much of the global humanitarian response infrastructure</a> that people would have relied on in the past to mitigate the impacts of such a crisis.</p> <p>In particular, the <a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/19/world/aid-cuts-somalia-putin-china.html?searchResultPosition=3\">closure of USAID</a> and the major cuts in spending on foreign aid â€“ not only by the U.S. but <a href=\"https://www.thenewhumanitarian.org/maps-and-graphics/2026/04/10/what-latest-oecd-numbers-tell-us-about-future-aid\">by most Western nations</a> â€“ has undermined much of the international systemâ€™s capacity to respond to major shocks. Combined with <a href=\"https://www.davidpublisher.com/Public/uploads/Contribute/6932442704428.pdf\">longer-term political and governance challenges in eastern Africa</a>, this conjunction of existing and anticipated factors in the Horn of Africa risks both further displacement and a rise in mortality. </p> <figure class=\"align-center \"> <img alt=\"A woman carries cans of water in front of a row of tents.\" src=\"https://images.theconversation.com/files/748015/original/file-20260715-69-mu71j0.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;q=45&amp;auto=format&amp;w=754&amp;fit=clip\" srcset=\"https://images.theconversation.com/files/748015/original/file-20260715-69-mu71j0.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;q=45&amp;auto=format&amp;w=600&amp;h=400&amp;fit=crop&amp;dpr=1 600w, https://images.theconversation.com/files/748015/original/file-20260715-69-mu71j0.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;q=30&amp;auto=format&amp;w=600&amp;h=400&amp;fit=crop&amp;dpr=2 1200w, https://images.theconversation.com/files/748015/original/file-20260715-69-mu71j0.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;q=15&amp;auto=format&amp;w=600&amp;h=400&amp;fit=crop&amp;dpr=3 1800w, https://images.theconversation.com/files/748015/original/file-20260715-69-mu71j0.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;q=45&amp;auto=format&amp;w=754&amp;h=503&amp;fit=crop&amp;dpr=1 754w, https://images.theconversation.com/files/748015/original/file-20260715-69-mu71j0.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;q=30&amp;auto=format&amp;w=754&amp;h=503&amp;fit=crop&amp;dpr=2 1508w, https://images.theconversation.com/files/748015/original/file-20260715-69-mu71j0.jpg?ixlib=rb-4.1.0&amp;q=15&amp;auto=format&amp;w=754&amp;h=503&amp;fit=crop&amp;dpr=3 2262w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1466px) 754px, (max-width: 599px) 100vw, (min-width: 600px) 600px, 237px\"> <figcaption> <span class=\"caption\">A Sudanese woman carries a jerry can of water at the Al-Afad camp for displaced people in northern Sudan on Nov. 21, 2025.</span> <span class=\"attribution\"><a class=\"source\" href=\"https://www.gettyimages.co.uk/detail/news-photo/sudanese-woman-who-fled-el-fasher-in-darfur-carries-news-photo/2247191335?adppopup=true\">Ebrahim Hamid/AFP via Getty Images</a></span> </figcaption> </figure> <p>There have <a href=\"https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(24)00426-4/abstract\">already been calls</a> by aid groups and regional bodies for higher levels of funding to be allocated through what remains of the international system.</p> <p>But by itself, more money cannot solve this problem. Humanitarian programs have been shut down, staff have been laid off and infrastructure sold. Government-led programs in epidemic tracking and the provision of healthcare and <a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12963-025-00376-y\">other areas have likewise been curtailed</a>.</p> <p>Washington has recently increased its level of funding slightly, but U.S. humanitarian assistance is now more politicized and transactional, less sufficient and <a href=\"https://alnap.org/help-library/resources/global-humanitarian-assistance-gha-report-2025-e-report/\">limited in its support of effective local systems</a> and responses.</p> <h2>Local efforts addressing the problem</h2> <p>With the international humanitarian system compromised by funding cuts, the onus will be more heavily on governments, civil society and local communities in the Horn to mitigate anticipated shocks and their fallout.</p> <p>The first responders in any crisis are usually next of kin, neighbors and local mutual aid groups. Since the collapse of the international aid system a year ago, long-standing efforts by local communities and organizations have ramped up significantly and effectively. These <a href=\"https://www.thenewhumanitarian.org/analysis/2026/01/28/amid-aid-cuts-regression-spontaneous-volunteerism\">local mutual aid efforts</a> are typically self-managed and financed through their own networks and diaspora remittances. But given the size of budget cuts in the formal humanitarian response system, these local initiatives can be better supported with complementary funding. Several intermediary organizations have <a href=\"https://www.proximity2humanity.org\">recently emerged to support this effort</a>.</p> <p>Local mutual aid efforts can also be supported through more flexible public policies, for example, by easing <a href=\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/?fr=RR-11&amp;ref=pdf_download&amp;rr=a176261309426cbc\">restrictions on banking transfers</a> to enable more effective diaspora support. Donors and the U.N. can also ensure that greater proportions of funding in the formal global humanitarian system go to local initiatives</p> <p>Fundamentally, however, the most worrisome driver behind this polycrisis is violent conflict â€“ wars in this region and elsewhere. In addition to the impacts of the conflicts in Ukraine and the Middle East, civil wars and ongoing insurgencies in Sudan, South Sudan, Ethiopia and Somalia have also been dragging on for years and in some cases have drawn in more countries â€“ either directly through financial support and <a href=\"https://www.swp-berlin.org/publikation/the-destabilising-role-of-the-united-arab-emirates-in-african-conflicts\">weapons sales or indirectly through political support</a>.</p> <p>Not only does violent conflict complicate any response to crisis, it also raises the risk of starvation being used as a weapon of war â€“ something already alleged in Sudan and the Tigray region of Ethiopia. But for the most part, the international community has been unwilling to hold warring parties and their foreign supporters accountable for <a href=\"https://theconversation.com/starvation-as-a-weapon-of-war-how-ethiopia-created-a-famine-in-tigray-268395\">intentionally starving parts of their populations</a>.</p> <p>Finally, data and monitoring systems have also been significantly crippled by aid cuts and <a href=\"https://unglobalriskreport.org/UNHQ-GlobalRiskReport-WEB-FIN.pdf\">misinformation</a>, making it difficult to understand the gravity of these crises or to keep actors accountable. Improved monitoring and evidence, including evidence of atrocities and the use of starvation as a weapon, need to be supported if there is to be effective assistance and accountability.</p> <p>The conjunction of so many potential hazards has all the hallmarks of the proverbial perfect storm. Not only will peopleâ€™s access to adequate food be devastated, it will also affect civilian protection, access and mobility. Without immediate prevention measures, the looming polycrisis will have widespread impacts on basic health, nutrition and water services â€“ and the potential for mass mortality and displacement.</p><img src=\"https://counter.theconversation.com/content/286071/count.gif\" alt=\"The Conversation\" width=\"1\" height=\"1\" /> <p class=\"fine-print\"><em><span>Daniel Maxwell is a member of the IPC Famine Review Committee, hosted by the UN Food and Agriculture Organization. Though now a Professor Emeritus, Daniel Maxwell was employed by Tufts university for 20 years and had numerous research grants from USAID.</span></em></p><p class=\"fine-print\"><em><span>Luka Kuol is affiliated with the University of Juba, South Sudan, as a professor, and the Sudd Institute, a national think tank in South Sudan, as a senior research fellow. He is also a member of the IPC Global Famine Review Committee. </span></em></p><p class=\"fine-print\"><em><span>Member of Famine Review Committee. </span></em></p><p class=\"fine-print\"><em><span>Alex de Waal and Merry Fitzpatrick do not work for, consult, own shares in or receive funding from any company or organization that would benefit from this article, and have disclosed no relevant affiliations beyond their academic appointment.</span></em></p>"},"time":{"asserted":"2026-07-18T04:08:29.665Z"},"force":"alleges","provenance":{"witness":"decolonization","citation":"https://theconversation.com/africas-greater-horn-region-is-facing-a-looming-polycrisis-fueled-by-conflict-prices-climate-and-disease-286071"},"status":"supported"},{"id":"published","proposition":{"predicate":"published","object":"2026-07-16T12:22:02Z"},"time":{"asserted":"2026-07-18T04:08:29.665Z"},"force":"alleges","provenance":{"witness":"decolonization","citation":"https://theconversation.com/africas-greater-horn-region-is-facing-a-looming-polycrisis-fueled-by-conflict-prices-climate-and-disease-286071"},"status":"supported"}]}},{"path":"decolonization/as-climate-and-conflict-collide-indigenous-leaders-confront-overlapping-crises-at-the-un.json","value":{"subject":"as-climate-and-conflict-collide-indigenous-leaders-confront-overlapping-crises-at-the-un","claims":[{"id":"title","proposition":{"predicate":"title","object":"As climate and conflict collide, Indigenous leaders confront overlapping crises at the UN"},"time":{"asserted":"2026-07-18T04:08:29.665Z"},"force":"alleges","provenance":{"witness":"decolonization","citation":"https://ictnews.org/news/as-climate-and-conflict-collide-indigenous-leaders-confront-overlapping-crises-at-the-un/"},"status":"supported"},{"id":"url","proposition":{"predicate":"url","object":"https://ictnews.org/news/as-climate-and-conflict-collide-indigenous-leaders-confront-overlapping-crises-at-the-un/"},"time":{"asserted":"2026-07-18T04:08:29.665Z"},"force":"alleges","provenance":{"witness":"decolonization","citation":"https://ictnews.org/news/as-climate-and-conflict-collide-indigenous-leaders-confront-overlapping-crises-at-the-un/"},"status":"supported"},{"id":"summary","proposition":{"predicate":"summary","object":"This story was originally published by Grist. Te Aniwaniwa Paterson & Dionne Phillips Grist This story is published through the Indigenous News Alliance. Although itâ€™s been nearly 20 years since more than 100 countries adopted the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples, the rights and lives of those it is meant to protect remain under constant threat from compounding crises. Indigenous lands are battered by&nbsp; record-breaking storms , dessicated by drought, and vanishing under rising seas. Amid these climate-driven threats, Indigenous peoples continue dying by the hundreds in wars and&nbsp; while defending their lands , even as they are&nbsp; persecuted by government officials .&nbsp; â€œClimate change, militarization, extractivism, and legal marginalization reinforce one another,â€ said Binota Moy Dhamai, who is Tripura from the Chittagong Hill Tracts in Bangladesh, and a former chair of the United Nations Expert Mechanism on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples, or&nbsp; EMRIP . This week, hundreds of Indigenous delegates gather in Geneva at the 19th annual gathering of that United Nations body to discuss implementing solutions to these abuses and push all countries to more proactively protect Indigenous peoples everywhere. EMRIP, one of three U.N. organizations focused exclusively on Indigenous peoples, is composed of seven Indigenous experts, each representing a different region. As part of the U.N. Human Rights Council, they focus on developing the data, recommendations, and advice to shape international standards regarding the fundamental rights of Indigenous peoples and support their enforcement.&nbsp; This yearâ€™s&nbsp; session &nbsp;will see Indigenous leaders, governments, and experts come together for a week of discussions and debates. The weekâ€™s meetings will cover both longstanding and emerging issues, including inadequate disaster relief, artificial intelligence, and resource extraction. For many Indigenous leaders around the world, the stakes could not be any higher, given the overlapping existential threats of violent conflict and climate change.&nbsp;&nbsp; â€œIndigenous peoples always face the brunt of conflict,â€ said Valmaine Toki, who is MÄori and the EMRIP member representing the Pacific region. Conflict, she said, leads to the dispossession of, and alienation from, their traditional lands even as the people from whom they are stolen are drawn into those fights. â€œItâ€™s a lose-lose situation for Indigenous peoples on all fronts.â€ This year, those meeting in Geneva face a growing list of intersecting issues, including militarization, climate change, exploitation by extractive industries, biodiversity decline, shrinking civic space, and digital transformation, Dhamai said.&nbsp; For Indigenous peoples in the Pacific, for instance, climate change and colonization are inextricably linked. Those from the South Pacific nation of New Caledonia have been part of the Pacific-led effort to hold large countries responsible for the climate crisis and its impact. Viro Xulue is Kanak and the Human Rights and Indigenous Rights advisor for the Drehu Customary Council, which is a traditional Kanak governance body. He said such work is part of the ongoing fight for true independence. â€œItâ€™s important for us in the process of decolonization because itâ€™s to respect the self-determination and what we want for our future, what we want for our children, and what we want to protect our resources, our moana, and our dream for the next generation,â€ Xulue said. Despite legal victories like an International Court of Justice&nbsp; advisory opinion &nbsp;that said the countries contributing to climate change are accountable for the damage it has caused, the Indigenous people of New Caledonia continue fighting for their inherent rights. â€œThe inequity for Kanak people is always here since 170 years of colonization,â€ Xulue said. â€œThe injustice is always here for the Indigenous Kanak. They didnâ€™t resolve the problem of the colonization and the impact of the colonization.â€&nbsp; Although the challenges facing Indigenous peoples vary across regions, those attending EMRIP plan to discuss several common themes. One of them is the ongoing fight for Indigenous involvement in government decision-making. Many countries do not follow international standards, and there is widespread agreement among Indigenous peoples that say they should be consulted and included in discussions that could impact them and their land.&nbsp; In northern Ontario, for example, the Canadian government has granted mining rights on the vast wetland ecosystem known as the Breathing Lands. It is part of the Ring of Fire mineral deposit and holds enormous stores of nickel and other metals. It is also a massive carbon sink and a key biodiversity hub. Extraction projects are proceeding despite strong resistance from First Nations in the area, including the Nishnawbe Aski Nation, that represents 49 communities. Kohen Mattinas of Lac Seul First Nation and Constance Lake First Nation is representing Nishnawbe Aski Nation at EMRIP and says that the right of all Indigenous peoples to free, prior, and informed consent must be respected. Mattinas is also the co-founder of Okiniwak, a youth organization created in 2025 in response to Canadian legislation that allows projects to be â€œadvanced through an accelerated process.â€ In addition to its international advocacy, Okiniwak organizes rallies in Indigenous communities and at legislative buildings. â€œWeâ€™ve taken it upon ourselves to protect the land and our rights on the front lines,â€ he said. For Mattinas, attending EMRIP is an important part of that work. â€œThrough speaking at these international forums, weâ€™re also able to connect with other Indigenous people around the world who are either facing the same things, or they face similar things, and we just kind of learn from each other, bring back that knowledge home, or we are able to collaborate in ways to strengthen both of our messaging or our work,â€ he said.&nbsp; Those connections can be even more valuable for Indigenous people from countries that do not recognize their legal status, like Botswana. Nichodimas Cooper, who is Indigenous Nama from that nation, said that lack of recognition makes it difficult to bring change on the national level. Cooper said his people face forced assimilation and displacement, and EMRIP offers a unique opportunity to pressure countries to respect their inalienable rights. â€œWe find it as a very important platform that empowers Indigenous peoples like us,â€ he said. â€œWe hardly have such a privilege as Indigenous communities to resource ourselves to attend these platforms and be able to inform ourselves on the mechanisms or platforms that are able to make impact within our advocacyâ€™s hope.â€ But as Indigenous delegates continue building solidarity, new threats, like artificial intelligence, are emerging. That has led to a push for data sovereignty and stronger protections against technologies being developed without free, prior, and informed consent.&nbsp; Dhamai said he hopes EMRIP will provide guidance on these mounting issues, alongside&nbsp; climate finance &nbsp;and the global energy transition, because Indigenous perspectives must shape international standards rather than respond to them.&nbsp; This yearâ€™s session is also expected to be shaped by reflections on the approaching&nbsp; 20th anniversary &nbsp;of UNDRIP next year. Nearly two decades after the declarationâ€™s adoption, the central question facing delegates remains remarkably similar to the one asked in 2007: not whether Indigenous rights should be recognized, but whether governments are prepared to uphold them. The post As climate and conflict collide, Indigenous leaders confront overlapping crises at 